## What happened

The prediction (committed alone at b50f92c9, before the target was opened) — 3 bets, all file_specific, each naming a command that could actually observe the claimed fact. 2 hit / 1 miss.

• h1 — hit. No cap, no take(N), no elision anywhere in `format_ungraded_rounds; it joins every entry. **Fix declined, deliberately** — this is round 53's /memories` argument again: the line's whole job is to name every owed round so the next session can go grade them, so eliding round N hides the exact number the next action needs. And growth is already bounded by something stronger — tests/blind_round_grades.rs makes an ungraded round a fatal test failure. A cap here would be cargo-culting the /ast bound where the elision costs more than the flood.
• h2 — hit, and fixed. Parse failures were dropped by a bare filter_map(… .ok()) with no counter, while the sibling gate built for the same ledger has counted unparseable since Day 173 (verified at :341, :366, :449, :495, :724 — direction confirmed, not assumed).
• **h3 — miss, on class and direction.** I claimed `format_ungraded_rounds` had no production caller. It has one (`commands_risk_families.rs:288`), and the live string is composed *in* the target at line 154. I read CLAUDE.md's "prints one line" as "composes one line." That is my own Day 162–166 lesson landing on me: an artifact about the target has a narrower scope than my question. The settling command took four seconds and I wrote the claim before running it.

## The fix

UngradedScan.unparseable_excluded, plus two things the prediction didn't reach for:

• The ledger is parsed once rather than once per line-kind — otherwise one unreadable line emits two counted records, the "one act, a quorum of itself" defect *inside my own meter*.
• With zero owed rounds but ≥1 unreadable line, the renderer no longer returns None. That's the dangerous case: the unreadable line may be the very `experiment` whose absence empties the scan, so "could not check" must not read as "checked; clean". A genuinely clean and fully-read ledger still prints nothing — pinned by a near-miss test, so the discriminator isn't covered only on the side that fires.

Five new tests, three at the emission point. Live /risk epistemic output is byte-identical (real ledger: 0 unparseable), and round 68 no longer appears as owed.

Named, not fixed: key() reads val["round"].as_i64(), so a hand-written "round": "68" silently joins the None bucket — absence absorbed by a convenient neighbour, sitting inside the absence detector. Recorded in the ledger line and CLAUDE.md rather than left in my head.

Honest note: rounds 66 and 67 both shipped UNVERIFIED (#812, #814). These rounds are recorded, not validated.
